DF-EP-001 — Enterprise Persistence Problem Statement (Draft for Discovery)

Status: Discovery (Not Yet Locked)

Purpose

Define why the HCGO platform requires Enterprise Persistence before identifying what shall be persisted or how persistence will be implemented.

This discovery establishes the business and architectural justification for Enterprise Persistence and becomes the traceability anchor for all subsequent Discovery, Analysis, Architecture, and Implementation activities.

Discovery
Enterprise Persistence is NOT Required Because BTA Needs a Database

The requirement for Enterprise Persistence is not driven by PostgreSQL, Commentary, CRM, or any individual feature.

Rather, Enterprise Persistence is required because HCGO is evolving from a single-domain application into a governed enterprise platform supporting multiple independent domains, governed participation, and long-term reader relationships.

Persistence therefore becomes a platform capability, not an application capability.

Enterprise Problems Being Solved
Problem 1 — Knowledge Must Persist Beyond Runtime

The current runtime architecture is intentionally transient.

QueryService orchestrates responses for a single interaction.

However, governed knowledge assets—including commentary, expert metadata, source registries, and future domain participants—must persist independently of individual requests.

Without Enterprise Persistence, these governed knowledge assets cannot scale across domains.

Problem 2 — Enterprise Assets Must Become Managed Resources

Many platform resources become enterprise assets rather than application artifacts.

Examples include:

Commentary corpus
Scholar metadata
Expert registries
Translation metadata
Source registries
Participation metadata
Governance metadata

These assets require lifecycle management independent of application code.

Problem 3 — Reader Relationships Extend Beyond Individual Sessions

HCGO is designed to support ongoing Human-Centered Exploration.

This requires persistence of reader-owned information that survives beyond a single interaction while remaining governed by Reader Sovereignty.

Examples include:

Reader Journey
Exploration continuity
CRM
Preferences
Testimonies
Consent
Problem 4 — Platform Growth Requires Domain Independence

Future HCGO domains will introduce additional governed knowledge participants.

Examples include:

Bible scholars
OSHA subject experts
Healthcare authorities
Legal authorities
Regulatory experts
Future domain specialists

Enterprise Persistence must support these participants through configuration rather than architectural redesign.

Problem 5 — Commercial Participation Requires Enterprise Governance

Future participation models introduce enterprise responsibilities including:

Public domain sources
Licensed sources
Subscription content
Permission-based participation
Organizational agreements

These relationships become corporate assets requiring governed persistence.

Problem 6 — Constitutional Governance Requires Persistent State

Human-Centered Governance extends beyond runtime orchestration.

Governance itself requires persistent configuration supporting:

Participation policies
Domain registration
Authorization metadata
Auditability
Traceability
Future governance evolution

Persistence supports governance while remaining subordinate to QueryService orchestration authority.

Discovery Observation

Enterprise Persistence is fundamentally different from traditional application persistence.

Traditional persistence primarily stores application data.

Enterprise Persistence stores governed enterprise assets that are:

reusable,
traceable,
independently managed,
constitutionally governed,
domain-independent.
Initial Discovery Conclusion

Enterprise Persistence exists to preserve governed enterprise state across the HCGO platform.

It is not introduced to satisfy a database requirement.

It is introduced because HCGO requires persistent management of governed knowledge, governed participation, reader continuity, and enterprise assets while preserving constitutional authority separation.

